OSKit NIC server — virtio-net front door (standalone boot module)
===================================================================

Minix 3-style server that fronts the single virtio-net driver.
Runs as an isolated boot-module process with its own page table,
its own copy of `virtio_net.lsysl`, and an MMIO grant that maps
virtio-mmio slot 31 (virtio-net) at a fixed VA inside the server.
Kernel code on the aarch64 side no longer links the driver.

DMA buffers (rings + RX/TX payload pages) live in the server's
own BSS. `virtio_dma_server.lsysl` provides the `vnet_va_to_pa`
primitive via `svc_vm_v2p`, so descriptors point the device at
the PA of each server-owned page.

Single-threaded (no user-facing `create_thread` yet). Two wake
sources feed the main `ipc_recv_notify` loop:

1. Platform virtio IRQ — the kernel's `virtio_handler()` acks the
   device-side INT_STATUS and fires `notify_send(nic_tid, 1)`.
   The nic registers for this once via `svc_virtio_set_notify` at
   startup; every subsequent used-ring update wakes us.
2. Client IPC — GET_MAC / SEND_PACKET / SUBSCRIBE_RX /
   RECV_PACKET.

Every wake runs `nic_drain_rx` first, so even rare IRQ misses
(e.g. if the notify target was unregistered) get caught the next
time a client pings the server. The subscriber (inet) gets a
single notification per drained batch.

IPC protocol
------------

Each request carries a 1-byte command code in `buf[0]`. Replies
always start with a 1-byte status (0 on success, -1 on error).

- GET_MAC: `cmd` → `status, mac(6)`
- SEND_PACKET: `cmd, len(2, big-endian), frame(len)` → `status`
- SUBSCRIBE_RX: `cmd` → `status`
    Registers the calling thread as the single RX notification
    target. Every subsequent drain that picks up a frame bumps
    a `notify_send_to(subscriber_tid, 1)` on the subscriber.
- RECV_PACKET: `cmd` → `status, len(2, big-endian), frame(len)`
    Non-blocking fetch of the head of the RX queue. `status = 0`
    + the frame on success; `status = -1` + `len = 0` when the
    queue is empty.

All multi-byte wire fields are big-endian to match the rest of
the stack. Unknown commands reply `status = -1`.

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.ipc.{port_create, port_register, ipc_recv_notify, ipc_reply}
    import oskit.services.{rs_ready, notify_send_to, notify_read_self,
        thread_id, svc_virtio_set_notify}
    import oskit.drivers.virtio.{virtio_net_init, virtio_net_get_mac,
        virtio_net_send, virtio_net_recv}
    import oskit.arch.srv_nic_attach


Command codes.

    val NIC_CMD_GET_MAC      = 1
    val NIC_CMD_SEND_PACKET  = 2
    val NIC_CMD_SUBSCRIBE_RX = 3
    val NIC_CMD_RECV_PACKET  = 4


Capacity. Ethernet II without VLAN caps at 1514 bytes; IPC
message buffer carries that plus a 3-byte header plus slop.

    val NIC_MAX_FRAME = 1514
    val NIC_MSG_BUF   = 1520


RX queue — fixed-size circular buffer. Eight slots is plenty for
bring-up; the size is constrained by the module-level storage
budget more than by any protocol concern.

    val NIC_RX_QUEUE = 8

    struct NicRxFrame
        data: [1514]byte
        len: int

    var nic_rx_queue: [8]NicRxFrame
    var nic_rx_head = 0
    var nic_rx_tail = 0
    var nic_rx_count = 0
    var nic_rx_drops = 0


Single subscriber for now — inet is the sole RX consumer.

    var nic_subscriber_tid = -1
    var nic_port = -1


### Byte-buffer helpers

    nic_read_u16(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff)


### RX drain

Pull frames out of the virtio-net RX ring and park them in the
internal queue. Called at the top of every IPC wake so each
request implicitly polls the wire. Drops frames silently when
the queue is full (UDP-ish semantics); bumps `nic_rx_drops` for
diagnostics.

    nic_drain_rx()
        var buf: [1514]byte
        var notified = 0
        while true
            val n = virtio_net_recv(buf, 1514)
            if n <= 0
                break
            if n > NIC_MAX_FRAME
                nic_rx_drops += 1
            else if nic_rx_count >= NIC_RX_QUEUE
                nic_rx_drops += 1
            else
                val slot = nic_rx_tail
                nic_rx_queue[slot].len = n
                var i = 0
                while i < n
                    nic_rx_queue[slot].data[i] = buf[i]
                    i += 1
                nic_rx_tail = (slot + 1) % NIC_RX_QUEUE
                nic_rx_count += 1
                notified = 1
        if notified != 0
            if nic_subscriber_tid >= 0
                notify_send_to(nic_subscriber_tid, 1)


### GET_MAC handler

    nic_handle_get_mac(sender: int)
        var reply: [7]byte
        var mac: [6]byte
        if virtio_net_get_mac(mac) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            var i = 0
            while i < 6
                reply[1 + i] = 0
                i += 1
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < 6
            reply[1 + i] = mac[i]
            i += 1
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)


### SEND_PACKET handler

    nic_handle_send_packet(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val len = nic_read_u16(buf, 1)
        var reply: [1]byte
        if len <= 0 || len > NIC_MAX_FRAME
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val frame: *byte = buf + 3
        val r = virtio_net_send(frame, len)
        if r == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
        else
            reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### SUBSCRIBE_RX handler

    nic_handle_subscribe_rx(sender: int)
        nic_subscriber_tid = sender
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### RECV_PACKET handler

Pop one frame off the head of the RX queue. Non-blocking: empty
queue returns `status = -1, len = 0`.

    nic_handle_recv_packet(sender: int)
        if nic_rx_count <= 0
            var empty: [3]byte
            empty[0] = byte(-1)
            empty[1] = 0
            empty[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, empty, 3)
            return
        val slot = nic_rx_head
        val len = nic_rx_queue[slot].len
        var reply: [1517]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte((len >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte(len & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < len
            reply[3 + i] = nic_rx_queue[slot].data[i]
            i += 1
        nic_rx_head = (slot + 1) % NIC_RX_QUEUE
        nic_rx_count -= 1
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3 + len)


### Server main loop

Attach the driver to the granted MMIO slot, bring up the device,
register port "nic", signal RS, then service IPC. Every IPC wake
implicitly drains the virtio RX ring first.

`virtio_net_init()` may fail (e.g. if QEMU was started without
virtio-net). We still register the port and call `rs_ready()` in
that case — inet's nic IPC wrappers degrade to returning -1 and
UDP loopback keeps working. Matches the inet "best effort" pattern.

    nic_server()
        srv_nic_attach()
        virtio_net_init()

        nic_port = port_create()
        port_register(nic_port, "nic")

Register this thread as the virtio IRQ notify target. On aarch64
the kernel's `virtio_handler()` acks the device and sends us a
notification whenever the used ring grows — `ipc_recv_notify`
below returns -2, we drain, back to sleep. Unregistered
(kernel-side -1 default) means polling-only; harmless on
builds without IRQ routing wired yet.

        svc_virtio_set_notify(thread_id())

        rs_ready()

        var buf: [1520]byte
        while 1 == 1
            val r = ipc_recv_notify(nic_port, buf, NIC_MSG_BUF)
            if r == -2
                notify_read_self()
                nic_drain_rx()
            else if r >= 0
                nic_drain_rx()
                val sender = r
                val cmd = int(buf[0]) & 0xff
                if cmd == NIC_CMD_GET_MAC
                    nic_handle_get_mac(sender)
                else if cmd == NIC_CMD_SEND_PACKET
                    nic_handle_send_packet(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == NIC_CMD_SUBSCRIBE_RX
                    nic_handle_subscribe_rx(sender)
                else if cmd == NIC_CMD_RECV_PACKET
                    nic_handle_recv_packet(sender)
                else
                    var reply: [1]byte
                    reply[0] = byte(-1)
                    ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
